Now let me look at the `FLAC__bitreader_read_rice_signed_block` function more carefully. The issue is in the main loop where it reads the binary LSBs. Let me trace through the logic:

In the main loop (the fast path), when reading the binary LSBs:
```c
x = (FLAC__uint32)(b >> (FLAC__BITS_PER_WORD - parameter));
if(parameter <= ucbits) {
    ucbits -= parameter;
    b <<= parameter;
} else {
    /* there are still bits left to read, they will all be in the next word */
    cwords++;
    if (cwords >= words)
        goto incomplete_lsbs;
    b = br->buffer[cwords];
    ucbits += FLAC__BITS_PER_WORD - parameter;
    x |= (FLAC__uint32)(b >> ucbits);
    b <<= FLAC__BITS_PER_WORD - ucbits;
}
```

The issue is: after reading the unary MSBs, `ucbits` is computed as `(ucbits - x - 1) % FLAC__BITS_PER_WORD`. If `ucbits` was 0 before the unary read, then `(0 - x - 1) % FLAC__BITS_PER_WORD` wraps around. But wait, `ucbits` is unsigned, so this is well-defined modulo arithmetic.

Actually, let me think about this differently. The crash is a heap-buffer-overflow READ 4. This means the bitreader is reading from `br->buffer[cwords]` where `cwords >= words`. Let me look at the `incomplete_msbs` and `incomplete_lsbs` labels more carefully.

In the main loop, when `cwords >= words`:
- For MSBs: it goes to `incomplete_msbs` which sets `br->consumed_bits = 0; br->consumed_words = cwords;` then calls `FLAC__bitreader_read_unary_unsigned` which may call `bitreader_read_from_client_` to refill the buffer.
- For LSBs: it goes to `incomplete_lsbs` which does the same.

But the issue is: after `process_tail` completes and we go back to the main loop, `cwords` and `words` are reloaded from `br->consumed_words` and `br->words`. But `ucbits` is set to `FLAC__BITS_PER_WORD - br->consumed_bits` and `b = br->buffer[cwords] << br->consumed_bits`.

Wait, I see a potential issue. After `process_tail`, when we go back to the main loop:
```c
cwords = br->consumed_words;
words = br->words;
ucbits = FLAC__BITS_PER_WORD - br->consumed_bits;
b = br->buffer[cwords] << br->consumed_bits;
```

If `cwords >= words` at this point, then `br->buffer[cwords]` is reading past the end of the buffer! But this is inside a `do { ... } while(cwords >= words && val < end)` loop, so it should only loop back if `cwords >= words`. But the issue is that `b = br->buffer[cwords]` is evaluated BEFORE the while condition check.

Actually wait, let me re-read the code. The `process_tail` section is:
```c
process_tail:
    do {
        if(0) {
incomplete_msbs:
            br->consumed_bits = 0;
            br->consumed_words = cwords;
        }
        /* read the unary MSBs and end bit */
        if(!FLAC__bitreader_read_unary_unsigned(br, &msbs))
            return false;
        msbs += x;
        x = ucbits = 0;

        if(0) {
incomplete_lsbs:
            br->consumed_bits = 0;
            br->consumed_words = cwords;
        }
        /* read the binary LSBs */
        if(!FLAC__bitreader_read_raw_uint32(br, &lsbs, parameter - ucbits))
            return false;
        lsbs = x | lsbs;

        /* compose the value */
        x = (msbs << parameter) | lsbs;
        *val++ = (int)(x >> 1) ^ -(int)(x & 1);
        x = 0;

        cwords = br->consumed_words;
        words = br->words;
        ucbits = FLAC__BITS_PER_WORD - br->consumed_bits;
        b = br->buffer[cwords] << br->consumed_bits;
    } while(cwords >= words && val < end);
```

The problem is clear now! After the `process_tail` loop processes a value, it reloads `cwords` and `words`. If `cwords >= words`, it loops back. But `b = br->buffer[cwords]` is executed BEFORE the while condition check. If `cwords >= words`, then `br->buffer[cwords]` reads past the end of the buffer!

But wait, this only happens when `cwords >= words`, meaning there are no more complete words in the buffer. The `br->buffer[cwords]` access would be out of bounds.

Actually, let me look more carefully. The `process_tail` loop condition is `cwords >= words && val < end`. So it loops when there are no more complete words AND there are still values to read. But `b = br->buffer[cwords]` is executed at the bottom of the loop body, before the condition check. If `cwords >= words`, then `cwords` is an index past the valid words, so `br->buffer[cwords]` is an out-of-bounds read.

But actually, this is the tail processing path. The real question is: can we get into the main loop with `cwords >= words`?

Let me look at the main loop entry:
```c
cwords = br->consumed_words;
words = br->words;

/* if we've not consumed up to a partial tail word... */
if(cwords >= words) {
    x = 0;
    goto process_tail;
}

ucbits = FLAC__BITS_PER_WORD - br->consumed_bits;
b = br->buffer[cwords] << br->consumed_bits;
```

So if `cwords >= words` at the start, we go directly to `process_tail`. Otherwise, we enter the main loop.

In the main loop, when reading MSBs:
```c
x = y = COUNT_ZERO_MSBS2(b);
if(x == FLAC__BITS_PER_WORD) {
    x = ucbits;
    do {
        cwords++;
        if (cwords >= words)
            goto incomplete_msbs;
        b = br->buffer[cwords];
        y = COUNT_ZERO_MSBS2(b);
        x += y;
    } while(y == FLAC__BITS_PER_WORD);
}
```

And when reading LSBs:
```c
if(parameter <= ucbits) {
    ucbits -= parameter;
    b <<= parameter;
} else {
    cwords++;
    if (cwords >= words)
        goto incomplete_lsbs;
    b = br->buffer[cwords];
    ucbits += FLAC__BITS_PER_WORD - parameter;
    x |= (FLAC__uint32)(b >> ucbits);
    b <<= FLAC__BITS_PER_WORD - ucbits;
}
```

So the main loop properly checks bounds before accessing `br->buffer[cwords]`. The issue must be in the `process_tail` path.

Let me think about what input would trigger this. We need a FLAC file where:
1. The blocksize is such that `partition_samples` is small
2. The Rice parameter causes the binary LSB part to straddle a word boundary
3. The bitreader buffer runs out of complete words

